{
  "result": "The best time to be in a grocery store is 9:47 PM on a Tuesday. The day has given up, the night hasn't really started.\n\nThe fluorescent lights buzz a little louder because there are fewer people to absorb the sound. Someone is restocking yogurt and you can hear every plastic tub click against the shelf. The music is some 80s song you vaguely remember from your mom's car, turned down low enough that it feels like it's playing just for you.\n\nI like to linger in the cereal aisle, even though I came for milk. There are too many colors there. Someone, at some point, decided that breakfast should be a cartoon tiger or a leprechaun, and we all just agreed.\n\nIt's in these places that I remember how strange it is to be a person. You have a favorite kind of apple. You have a brand of toothpaste you will defend to the death. You have a list in your pocket that says eggs, bread, tortillas, and that list is, for the next twenty minutes, your entire purpose.\n\nAnd then you leave. The automatic doors sigh open. The parking lot is cold. Your bags rustle. You drive home under streetlights that turn on one by one, as if they were watching you go.\n\nNothing happened. And everything did. \n\nThat's the quiet contract we make with the world: keep moving, keep buying milk, and let the small moments hold us together until morning, even when no one is looking.",
